        "question": {
          ":question/id": "22e9a117-5012-516a-98ae-ed5e59b50d0e",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
          ":question/problem": "The matrices $F$ and $A$ differ in just one entry. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nF=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "A square matrix is singular if and only if its determinant is zero. A singular matrix has no multiplicative inverse; a nonsingular square matrix has one. Compare both determinants rather than assuming that one changed entry changes singularity.\n\nExpand along the top row, with signs $+,-,+$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\det(F) &= 1\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&\\quad +(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&\\quad +(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 1\\left(1-(-2)\\right) \\\\\n&\\quad +(-2)\\left(1-(-1)\\right) \\\\\n&\\quad +(-1)\\left(-2-(-1)\\right) \\\\\n&= 1\\cdot 3 \\\\\n&\\quad +(-2)\\cdot 2 \\\\\n&\\quad +(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\\n&= 3-4+1 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\det(A) &= 1\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&\\quad +(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&\\quad +(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 1\\left(1-(0)\\right) \\\\\n&\\quad +(-2)\\left(1-(-1)\\right) \\\\\n&\\quad +(-1)\\left(0-(-1)\\right) \\\\\n&= 1\\cdot 1 \\\\\n&\\quad +(-2)\\cdot 2 \\\\\n&\\quad +(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 1-4-1 \\\\\n&= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(F)=0$, $F$ is singular.\n\nSince $\\det(A)=-4\\ne0$, $A$ is **not** singular.\n\nDo not select \u201cNone of the above.\u201d\n\nDo not select \u201c$F$ is **not** a singular matrix.\u201d\n\nDo not select \u201c$\\det(A)=2$.\u201d\n\nDo not select \u201c$F$ has a multiplicative inverse, but $A$ does not.\u201d\n\nSelect \u201c$A$ is **not** a singular matrix.\u201d",
